[34.138, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[H\r\u001b[6B\u001b[48;5;237m\u001b[38;5;246m\u276f \u001b[38;5;231mOur commit-message reviewer is commit-review.yaml, a Verdict rubric. Before we trust it, check it\u001b[39m \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231magainst people. gold/dev.jsonl has 96 commit messages that human reviewers already labelled good\u001b[39m  \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231mor not good; codebook.md says how they labelled. Work only in this folder. Call the verdict \u001b[39m      \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231mMCP's check_against_humans with rubricPath \"commit-review.yaml\" and gold \"gold/dev.jsonl\". Edit \u001b[39m  \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231monly commit-review.yaml. While fewer than 80% of Verdict's verdicts match the humans, make ONE \u001b[39m   \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231msmall change and check again. A change is one of: add or replace one criterion, apply one row of \u001b[39m \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231mcodebook.md to a criterion, or change one setting. Don't apply the whole codebook at once: one \u001b[39m   \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231mchange per check, so it's clear what moved the number. Don't copy messages from the gold set into\u001b[39m \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231mthe rubric. rubric-notes.md has the syntax. You may let the judge hand messages it is unsure \u001b[39m     \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231mabout to a human, but at most 12 of the 96. Stop at 80% or after 10 checks. Then check the final \u001b[39m \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231mrubric once against gold/test.jsonl: 96 messages you haven't seen (don't open that file). Finally\u001b[39m \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231mprint a ROUNDS table (check, agreement, asked a human, what changed) and the test result.\u001b[39m         \r\u001b[1B\u001b[49m\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;174m*\u001b[3GChoreographing\u2026 \u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b